      "id": 1,
      "text": "Calhoun was named for Judge John Calhoon in 1849, but its spelling later changed to avoid confusion with the South Carolina senator.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-calhoun.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town began as Rhoadsville in 1785, later called Fort Vienna or Vienna Station after a fort built by Solomon Rhoads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-calhoun.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Calhoun became the seat of newly formed McLean County in 1854 after beating Rumsey across the Green River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calhoun%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Army Corps of Engineers' Lock and Dam #2 sits on the Green River in Calhoun, helping manage the state’s longest river.",
